 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "birmingham_balti",
   "title": "The Balti",
   "text": "Birmingham's Pakistani and Kashmiri cooks made the balti famous from the 1970s: a curry cooked fast over a high flame and served sizzling in the thin steel bowl it was cooked in, with naan to scoop it up. Sparkbrook, Balsall Heath and Moseley became the Balti Triangle. Its exact origins are argued over, usually over a balti.",
   "people": "south_asian"
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "LIBRARY OF BIRMINGHAM. Opened in 2013, wrapped in metal rings like a giant piece of jewellery, which suits the city. It has a rooftop garden, a Shakespeare Memorial Room and more books than you could read."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "CENTENARY SQUARE. The city's big public living room, with fountains, statues, and a very fine view of the library. Brummies will argue about what the statues are called. They will also offer you tea during the argument."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "THE BULL. A giant bronze bull has stood outside the Bullring since 2003. The Bullring has been a market since 1166, so the bull is, if anything, a bit late. Please do not ride it. People have tried."
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "ST MARTIN IN THE BULLRING. A church has stood here for around 800 years, and the market has been bustling round it ever since. Choirs, bells, and a very patient vicar."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "SELFRIDGES. A building covered in 15,000 shiny aluminium discs, which look like a very expensive sequinned armadillo. It is the most photographed building in the city, and it has never once complained."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "THE ROTUNDA. A round 1960s office tower, saved from demolition and turned into flats. Birmingham has more canal mileage than Venice, and a surprising number of round buildings."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "BIRMINGHAM'S CANALS. The city has about 35 miles of canal, which is more than Venice. Narrowboats still drift through the heart of town. They have right of way. They also have tea."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "THE CUSTARD FACTORY. Once the Bird's Custard works, built in 1902. Alfred Bird, a Birmingham chemist, had invented egg-free custard in 1837 so his wife, who couldn't eat eggs, could join in the pudding. A kind idea, in a bowl."
  },
  {
   "x": 51,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "BACK-TO-BACK HOUSES. Birmingham once had thousands of homes built in rows, sharing walls on three sides. A few survive as a museum. They are small, snug, and rather harder to get a sofa into than they look."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "THE JEWELLERY QUARTER. About 40% of all the jewellery made in Britain still comes from this one square mile. The clock tower is a memorial and a great place to say 'meet you under the clock'."
  },
  {
   "x": 1,
   "y": 25,
   "text": "Road to Wolverhampton closed for resurfacing. Again. Sorry for any inconvenience."
  },
  {
   "x": 61,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "EAST: the A44 to the Cotswolds and Oxford. (Coventry is closed for resurfacing. Again. Sorry for any inconvenience.)"
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: J. R. R. Tolkien, author of The Hobbit, grew up in Birmingham from 1895, and the Shire owes a lot to Sarehole Mill.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "birmingham_tolkien",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "J. R. R. Tolkien"
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 17,
   "plaque": "birmingham_name",
   "title": "Name: Birmingham",
   "text": "Old English Beormingaham: the homestead of the people of Beorma, a Saxon leader we know nothing else about. In local speech it wore down over the centuries to Brummagem, which is where Brum and Brummie come from."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "birmingham_lunar_society",
   "title": "The Lunar Society",
   "text": "In the late 1700s a club of thinkers met around Birmingham: Matthew Boulton, James Watt, Erasmus Darwin, Joseph Priestley and Josiah Wedgwood among them. They met near the full moon so they could ride home by its light, and called themselves the Lunaticks."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 48,
   "plaque": "birmingham_chamberlain",
   "title": "Joseph Chamberlain",
   "text": "As mayor in the 1870s, Joseph Chamberlain bought out the town's gas and water companies, cleared slums and built Corporation Street. Admirers called it gas and water socialism. The Chamberlain Clock in the Jewellery Quarter was put up in his honour."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "birmingham_heavy_metal",
   "title": "Birth of Heavy Metal",
   "text": "Black Sabbath formed in Aston, Birmingham, in 1968, and are widely credited with inventing heavy metal. Their guitarist had lost two fingertips in a sheet metal factory and played on homemade thimbles. Birmingham industry, turned up to eleven."
  }
 ],
